Sarcoidosis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, Erythema nodosum, Non-caseating granulomas

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-041
Reason for Visit: Acute eruption of severely painful, bruise-like nodules on the lower legs preventing ambulation, combined with worsening exertional shortness of breath and intense eye pain.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient experiencing rapid functional decline due to severe lower extremity pain (erythema nodosum), accompanied by acute visual changes (photophobia) and progressive dyspnea. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to differentiate the respiratory symptoms from an acute pulmonary embolism, evaluate the eye for sight-threatening anterior uveitis, and provide immediate systemic analgesia, as delaying until standard hours posed significant risks of permanent visual impairment and unmanageable distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My shins have broken out in these massive, hot red lumps that hurt so much I can't even stand, my left eye is burning, and I'm constantly out of breath."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious onset of progressive exertional dyspnea over the past month, initially attributed to poor fitness, alongside a chronic, dry, non-productive cough. Over the last 48 hours, they experienced an acute eruption of erythema nodosum, presenting as exquisitely tender, bruised-appearing nodules on the anterior shins bilaterally. Today, the patient developed intense photophobia, blurred vision, and a red left eye (anterior uveitis). Also complains of symmetrical polyarthralgias most commonly affecting the ankles and knees, making weight-bearing agonizing. Reports generalized fatigue and an unintentional 3kg weight loss over the past 6 weeks. Denies any recent facial drooping (transient facial nerve palsy) or violaceous facial plaques (lupus pernio).
Functional Status: Housebound; mobilizing with extreme difficulty due to severe shin and ankle pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of falls and inability to perform basic self-care due to pain and visual disturbance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears fatigued, squinting the left eye, and sitting with legs elevated to minimize pain.
Physical Exam: Dermatological: Multiple (4-6 per leg), highly tender, poorly demarcated, erythematous, warm subcutaneous nodules on the anterior aspect of bilateral lower legs, consistent with erythema nodosum. Older lesions show a contusiform (bruise-like) evolution. HEENT: Left eye exhibits marked ciliary flush, conjunctival injection, and a sluggish pupillary response, highly suspicious for acute anterior uveitis. Respiratory: Mildly tachypneic at rest. Lung fields are clear to auscultation, but respiratory effort is increased. Musculoskeletal: Bilateral ankle joints are mildly swollen with periarticular tenderness. The diagnostic tetrad is heavily supported clinically: acute erythema nodosum, likely underlying bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy causing the dyspnea, and anticipating the discovery of non-caseating granulomas on future tissue biopsy.
Environmental Observations: Blinds drawn due to photophobia; environment safe for current limited mobility.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol with no relief of the mechanical shin pain or eye burning.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Sarcoidosis - Status: Exacerbated (Löfgren's syndrome variant with acute uveitis)
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly systemic, granulomatous presentation (uveitis, erythema nodosum, ankle arthritis) which perfectly defines acute sarcoidosis (Löfgren's syndrome) rather than an isolated vascular thrombotic event.

Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of significant hemoptysis, night sweats, or known exposure, combined with the acute, explosive onset of erythema nodosum and uveitis which strongly favors a sarcoid flare, though a chest X-ray is required.

Streptococcal Infection (Triggering Erythema Nodosum) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent pulmonary symptoms (dyspnea, dry cough) and the acute uveitis, indicating a wider systemic granulomatous disease rather than isolated post-streptococcal cutaneous inflammation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe flare of systemic sarcoidosis, characterized by the triad of erythema nodosum, polyarthralgia, and acute anterior uveitis, set against a background of progressive dyspnea. The emergence of intense eye pain and blurred vision constitutes an ophthalmic emergency. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely mandated to physically confirm the uveitis, evaluate the respiratory status, and instigate immediate high-dose systemic anti-inflammatories while arranging an urgent ophthalmology review. Deferring this assessment until standard daytime hours carried an imminent risk of irreversible synechiae formation and permanent vision loss, satisfying the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate a scheduled, high-dose NSAID regimen (e.g., Ibuprofen 400mg TID) to aggressively manage the painful erythema nodosum and polyarthralgia. The patient requires an immediate, urgent referral to an ophthalmologist (within 12-24 hours) for slit-lamp evaluation and prescription of topical corticosteroid/mydriatic drops for the uveitis. Arrange an urgent GP follow-up for a chest X-ray (to confirm bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy) and baseline bloods (including serum ACE).
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict ocular rest: Wear dark sunglasses indoors at all times and avoid all screen time; demonstrating that physically blocking ambient light immediately reduces the throbbing, photophobic pain in the eye, actively reinforcing the need to rest the inflamed iris. Leg elevation protocol: Keep both legs elevated above the level of the hips when seated; confirming that reducing the gravitational blood pooling visibly decreases the throbbing pain in the shin nodules, ensuring compliance with physical rest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent ophthalmology review. Routine GP review within 48 hours for imaging.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the vision in the left eye suddenly goes completely black, if the eye pain becomes agonizing and unremitting, or if you develop sudden, severe shortness of breath at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The combination of the shin rash, eye pain, and breathlessness strongly points to an inflammatory condition called Sarcoidosis. While the rash will settle, the inflammation in your eye requires immediate specialist treatment to protect your vision.
Clinical Course: With anti-inflammatory medication, the acute rash and joint pain typically resolve over several weeks. The eye requires specific steroid drops. The lung symptoms require a chest X-ray to determine if long-term oral steroids are needed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must be seen by an eye specialist as soon as possible. If your vision suddenly darkens entirely, or if you feel like you cannot catch your breath even while sitting still, you must go directly to the emergency department.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system has formed small clusters of inflammatory cells (granulomas) in your lungs, skin, and eyes. The strong anti-inflammatories will chemically force the swelling down in your legs, but the delicate tissue inside your eye needs direct treatment with specialized drops from an ophthalmologist.
Actionable Steps: Take the NSAIDs strictly with meals. Wear sunglasses indoors to reduce eye pain. Keep your legs elevated. Proceed to the GP or eye hospital tomorrow morning without fail.

